{% set local_problem %}
<p>Here's what we found on a house off McCord Avenue last month: homeowner wanted new gutters. When we pulled the old ones down, 26 feet of fascia was soft enough to push a finger through. The rot went back 3/4 inch in some spots - you could see the rafter tails behind it.</p>

<p>The homeowner had no idea. The paint looked okay. The gutters seemed attached. But water had been seeping behind those gutters for probably 8-10 years, slowly turning the wood to mush. If we'd installed new gutters without fixing the fascia, they'd have sagged within a year - screws don't hold in rotten wood.</p>

<p>This is common in Albertville. Houses from the 60s, 70s, and 80s with original gutters that leaked at seams for years before anyone noticed. Newer homes with builder-grade gutters that weren't installed quite right. Alabama humidity that keeps everything damp and makes rot spread faster.</p>

<p>We check fascia on every gutter estimate. Not because we're trying to upsell - because installing gutters on rotten wood is a waste of your money and our reputation.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Here's our actual process, not the abbreviated version:</p>

<p><strong>1. Thorough inspection.</strong> We don't just glance at the fascia - we probe it. Screwdriver test at 3-foot intervals along the entire gutter line. Looking for soft spots, water stains, paint failures, gaps. We'll show you exactly what we find.</p>

<p><strong>2. Remove old gutters.</strong> Can't assess or repair fascia with gutters in the way. Everything comes down first.</p>

<p><strong>3. Cut out the rot.</strong> We don't just cover it up. Damaged sections get removed completely - cut back to solid wood. If rot has spread to rafter tails, we address that too (additional work, but better to know now).</p>

<p><strong>4. Install new fascia.</strong> We use 1x6 or 1x8 primed finger-joint pine for most repairs - it's stable, takes paint well, and handles Alabama weather. For higher-end jobs or problem areas, we can use composite fascia that won't rot at all.</p>

<p><strong>5. Seal and prep.</strong> New fascia gets primed ends (where we cut it) and back-primed if it's going against unconditioned space. Proper prep prevents future rot.</p>

<p><strong>6. Install gutters.</strong> Now we've got solid wood to screw into. Gutters go up right, stay up, and work like they should.</p>

<p>Check out some of our <a href="/recent-projects/">recent fascia repair jobs</a> in Marshall County.</p>
{% endset %}

